.TH WISP 8 "2026-09-15" "wisp 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
wisp \- build a reproducible initramfs from declared inputs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wisp build
.RI [ \-k " version" ]
.RI [ \-o " file" ]
.RB [ \-\-compress
.IR none | zstd ]
.RI [ \-\-manifest " file" ]
.RI [ \-\-stage " dir" ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.br
.B wisp manifest
.RI [ \-k " version" ]
.RI [ \-\-stage " dir" ]
.br
.B wisp resolve
.RI [ \-k " version" ]
.IR selector ...
.br
.B update-initramfs
.BR \-c | \-u | \-d
.RI [ \-k " version" | all ]
.RI [ \-b " dir" ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wisp
assembles an initramfs whose bytes are a pure function of a manifest and the
kernel package's module tree. It does not scan the running machine for
loaded modules, does not copy binaries found by their shared libraries, and
does not stamp file system times into the archive. Two machines with the same
kernel package and the same declared inputs produce the same bytes.
.PP
The initramfs holds one static program as
.IR /init ,
.BR wisp-init (7),
the resolved kernel modules, and the measured configuration
.BR wisp.conf (5).
.PP
The same binary is installed as
.B /usr/sbin/update-initramfs
and as the kernel maintainer hooks under
.IR /etc/kernel/postinst.d " and " /etc/kernel/postrm.d ,
so kernel packages and other software keep working as with initramfs-tools.
.SH VERBS
.TP
.B build
Assemble the manifest for the kernel (see
.BR wisp.conf (5)
for the inputs), resolve the modules, and write the archive to
.IR /boot/initrd.img\- version
(or
.BR \-o ).
The output is written to a temporary file and renamed, so a failed build
leaves the previous initrd untouched. With
.B \-\-manifest
an explicit manifest is built instead of assembling one. With
.B \-\-stage
the assembled manifest and every generated file are kept in
.IR dir ,
which is what a verifier needs to rebuild the same bytes elsewhere.
.B \-v
lists the modules that went in. The compressed size, the SHA-256 of the
output and the SHA-256 of the uncompressed archive are printed.
.TP
.B manifest
Print the manifest that
.B build
would use.
.TP
.B resolve
Show the module resolution for names and selectors against
.IR /lib/modules/ version :
the files in load order, the names that are built into the kernel, and any
that are missing.
.TP
.B update-initramfs
The initramfs-tools compatible facade.
.B \-c
and
.B \-u
build,
.B \-d
deletes;
.B \-k
selects a version or
.BR all ,
the default is the newest installed kernel;
.B \-b
selects the boot directory.
.B \-t
is accepted and ignored. Unknown options are an error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/wisp/wisp.conf
The runtime configuration, completed by the builder and placed in the initramfs.
.TP
.I /etc/wisp/modules
Module names and directory selectors; a built-in list is used when absent.
.TP
.I /etc/wisp/lvm.tab
A hand-maintained volume table; otherwise recorded from the running system.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/wisp/key/
Key providers, see
.BR wisp.conf (5).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on any failure. The kernel hooks return 2 when called without a version.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wisp.conf (5),
.BR wisp-init (7)
